  "id": "Compound::DB00539 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Toremifene is implicated in the pathogenesis of breast cancer through a mechanism involving the oestrogenic effect, which is mediated by antiestrogen compounds. This pathway is supported by multiple evidence chains, including its role in causing oestrogenic effects, which are driven by anastrozole or letrozole, both of which are antisteroids. These compounds are known to inhibit oestrogen receptor activity, and their association with breast cancer suggests a potential therapeutic role. The shared lowest common ancestors—Antiestrogen and Organic Aromatic Compound—highlight a common biological pathway, reinforcing the link between Toremifene and the disease.\n\nThe evidence also reveals that Toremifene can contribute to breast cancer through a different route, via its association with uterine polyps, which are treated by tamoxifen. This suggests that Toremifene may have a broader impact on breast cancer through multiple mechanisms, potentially involving both oestrogenic and non-oestrogenic pathways. The integration of these pathways underscores the complexity of the relationship, with Toremifene acting as a bridge between antiestrogen compounds and breast cancer, though the exact nature of its direct involvement remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of multiple evidence chains, while not fully resolving all uncertainties, provides a robust foundation for considering Toremifene as a candidate for drug repositioning in breast cancer.",
